steps.text:
A [meta-analysis](g:meta-analysis) of 27 studies (swimmers, cyclists, runners) found the biggest gains from [[c:taper-bosquet]]:

- **volume** cut by **41–60%**;
- **intensity** kept;
- **frequency** (sessions per week) kept;
- about **2 weeks** long.

A 2023 meta-analysis of 14 studies in endurance athletes found the same: a 41–60% volume cut with intensity and frequency kept, and 8–14 days gave the largest effect, though only two data sets used that length [[c:taper-wang]].

Both pooled mostly before-and-after changes in the same athletes rather than comparisons with a control group [[c:taper-design]], so we grade this moderate, not strong.

What changes during a taper [[c:taper-mech]]:

- **fatigue clears**: models show the taper mostly restores capacities that heavy training had held down;
- **blood**: more blood and red cell volume, and more haemoglobin;
- **fuel**: muscle [glycogen](g:glycogen) rises;
- **muscle**: strength and power rise markedly;
- **mind**: better mood and sleep, lower perceived effort.

What often **doesn't** change: [VO2max](g:vo2max). A rise isn't needed for a better race [[c:taper-vo2]], and the 2023 meta-analysis found no average change in VO2max or economy, while time trials and time to exhaustion improved.

So the taper reveals fitness that fatigue was hiding more than it adds new fitness.
